The two terms under the radical share a factor of \(t^2\). Factoring exposes a square whose nonnegative square root is \(|t|\). For every real \(t\), both \(t^2\) and \(t^2+1\) are nonnegative, so the square-root product rule gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{t^4+t^2}
&=\sqrt{t^2(t^2+1)}\\
&=\sqrt{t^2}\sqrt{t^2+1}\\
&=|t|\sqrt{1+t^2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The absolute value ensures the result is nonnegative even when \(t<0\); the identity also holds at \(t=0\). Thus the answer is choice E.
